1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,504
2, 400 years ago, people in the
Iranian desert were making ice.

2
00:00:04,942 --> 00:00:08,022
In summer. With no
electricity. No machines.

3
00:00:08,418 --> 00:00:10,184
They served frozen desserts,

4
00:00:10,204 --> 00:00:13,713
in a place where July heat
tops 100 degrees Fahrenheit.

5
00:00:14,393 --> 00:00:16,128
And some of those buildings
are still standing.

6
00:00:16,917 --> 00:00:18,660
Today, Iran is short of water.

7
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:21,677
And the answer may be
sitting under its own desert.

8
00:00:22,305 --> 00:00:24,162
I'll show you three inventions.

9
00:00:24,182 --> 00:00:26,160
One makes ice. One cools a house.

10
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:28,037
And one drinks from a mountain.

11
00:00:28,057 --> 00:00:30,781
The third is the secret
behind the other two.

12
00:00:31,616 --> 00:00:34,500
This is Iran. Close to 90 million people.

13
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:36,078
The capital is Tehran.

14
00:00:36,891 --> 00:00:41,065
Invention one: the yakhchal.
In Persian, it means ice pit.

15
00:00:41,659 --> 00:00:45,134
It is a giant dome of mud brick,
with a deep cellar underneath.

16
00:00:45,759 --> 00:00:47,837
Beside it stands a tall shade wall.

17
00:00:47,857 --> 00:00:49,776
Shallow pools sit in that shade.

18
00:00:50,414 --> 00:00:51,899
On freezing winter nights,

19
00:00:51,919 --> 00:00:54,966
the clear desert sky pulls
the heat out of the water.

20
00:00:54,986 --> 00:00:56,259
By morning, it is ice.

21
00:00:57,005 --> 00:01:00,982
Workers cut the ice, packed it in
straw, and stored it in the cellar.

22
00:01:01,002 --> 00:01:03,358
A chimney at the top let warm air escape,

23
00:01:03,378 --> 00:01:05,521
so the cellar stayed cold all summer.

24
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:09,106
Popular accounts date
this idea to about 400 B.

25
00:01:09,126 --> 00:01:12,592
C. The oldest buildings still
standing are much younger.

26
00:01:12,612 --> 00:01:14,106
But the idea is ancient.

27
00:01:15,013 --> 00:01:19,363
That ice went into faloodeh, a frozen
dessert of thin noodles, rose water,

28
00:01:19,383 --> 00:01:22,100
and lime. People in
Shiraz still eat it today.

29
00:01:22,768 --> 00:01:26,149
But ice was a luxury. Everyone
else just had to survive the heat.

30
00:01:26,881 --> 00:01:30,630
Invention two: the badgir,
the Persian breeze catcher.

31
00:01:31,218 --> 00:01:34,394
Look at the rooftops of Yazd,
and you will see tall towers.

32
00:01:34,414 --> 00:01:35,931
About seven hundred of them.

33
00:01:36,386 --> 00:01:39,357
Each tower catches the
breeze high above the city,

34
00:01:39,377 --> 00:01:41,809
and sends cooler air down into the house.

35
00:01:41,829 --> 00:01:43,783
The hot air leaves through slots.

36
00:01:43,803 --> 00:01:44,939
The house breathes.

37
00:01:45,370 --> 00:01:49,425
The tallest one, at Dowlat Abad
Garden, is almost 34 meters high.

38
00:01:49,867 --> 00:01:52,513
Yazd is now a UNESCO World Heritage city.

39
00:01:52,533 --> 00:01:56,630
But Yazd gets almost no rain. So
where did its water come from?

40
00:01:57,346 --> 00:01:59,082
Invention three: the qanat.

41
00:01:59,584 --> 00:02:03,774
A qanat is a tunnel. It starts
underground, at the foot of a mountain,

42
00:02:03,794 --> 00:02:05,598
where water hides in the soil.

43
00:02:05,714 --> 00:02:09,246
The tunnel slopes just a little,
and gravity does the rest.

44
00:02:09,266 --> 00:02:13,540
The water travels kilometers, with
no pump, and no sun to evaporate it.

45
00:02:14,346 --> 00:02:19,337
The Gonabad qanat has 427 shafts,
over 33 kilometers of tunnel.

46
00:02:19,357 --> 00:02:22,360
It was dug more than 2, 500 years ago.

47
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:24,607
And it still delivers water.

48
00:02:25,256 --> 00:02:27,117
That water filled the ice pools.

49
00:02:27,137 --> 00:02:29,526
It cooled the houses. It fed the gardens.

50
00:02:29,546 --> 00:02:31,662
One invention powered the other two.

51
00:02:32,482 --> 00:02:36,656
So what went wrong? In recent
decades, Iran built about 600 dams,

52
00:02:36,676 --> 00:02:38,160
and drilled deep wells.

53
00:02:38,597 --> 00:02:43,368
Qanats were left behind. About 33,
000 still work, down from 50, 000.

54
00:02:43,388 --> 00:02:47,277
Hydrologists say about half
of the systems have run dry.

55
00:02:47,893 --> 00:02:49,889
And the dams? In November 2025,

56
00:02:49,909 --> 00:02:53,790
the five reservoirs feeding
Tehran averaged around 10% full.

57
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:57,647
The president has warned that
the capital may have to move.

58
00:02:58,442 --> 00:03:01,928
The ancient system kept desert
cities alive for 25 centuries.

59
00:03:01,948 --> 00:03:05,684
Whether the modern one can do the
same is still an open question.

60
00:03:06,677 --> 00:03:10,246
Canada currently advises against
all travel to Iran, so for now,

61
00:03:10,266 --> 00:03:11,556
we visit through video.

62
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:13,671
Which country should we visit next?

63
00:03:13,691 --> 00:03:14,713
Tell me in the comments.

